  teach:[{"title": "What performance data tells you", "mins": 10, "criteria": "D.P5", "explain": "Performance data is any information that describes how you played. Some of it is numbers the game records for you, such as kills, deaths and assists, accuracy, and time spent on the objective. Some of it is footage: a VOD is a recording of the match that you watch back. Numbers tell you what happened. Footage tells you why it happened. Coaches use both together, because a number on its own can mislead. A support player can have a low KDA and still be the reason the team won.", "points": ["KDA sums up kills, deaths and assists. A common formula is (kills + assists) ÷ deaths", "Accuracy is the percentage of your shots that hit", "Objective time is how long you spent on the point, payload or site", "A VOD review means watching the match back to see the decisions behind the numbers"], "terms": [{"term": "KDA", "meaning": "Kills, deaths and assists, often shown as a ratio"}, {"term": "VOD", "meaning": "Video on demand: a recording of a match you can watch back"}, {"term": "Scrim", "meaning": "A practice match arranged against another team"}], "example": "A player finishes with 4 kills, 2 deaths and 10 assists. Their KDA is (4 + 10) ÷ 2 = 7, even though they got very few kills themselves.", "ask": "Why might the player with the most kills still be the reason their team lost?", "answer": "They chased kills and left the objective, or died at key moments. Kills alone don't show decisions or teamwork."}, {"title": "Turning data into strengths and weaknesses", "mins": 10, "criteria": "D.P6", "explain": "One match proves very little. Anyone can have a good or a bad game, so analysts look for patterns: the same result in two or more matches. A strength is something the data shows you do well again and again. A weakness is something that costs your team again and again. Every claim needs evidence, which means a number or a timestamp from the VOD. Then ask what the weakness costs the team. Dying first in most fights matters more than slightly low accuracy, because the team has to fight with a player missing.", "points": ["Look for patterns across at least two matches, not one-offs", "Back every strength and weakness with a number or a VOD timestamp", "Judge a weakness by what it costs the team, not by how bad it looks", "Compare like with like: same game, same role, similar opponents"], "terms": [{"term": "Pattern", "meaning": "The same result showing up in more than one match"}, {"term": "Evidence", "meaning": "The number, screenshot or timestamp that proves your point"}, {"term": "Impact", "meaning": "The effect on your team's chance of winning"}], "example": "In both matches a player's accuracy is around 40%, but they also die first in most team fights. The deaths are the bigger weakness: each one leaves the team a player down.", "ask": "Your accuracy was 55% in one match and 31% in the next. Is accuracy a weakness?", "answer": "You can't tell yet. The two matches disagree, so check a third or watch the VOD to see what changed."}],
